# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction {#introduction}"
msgstr "はじめに {#introduction}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the Qiskit in the Classroom modules, you'll have the opportunity to use a quantum computer to explore various concepts in quantum computing-adjacent fields such as quantum mechanics, computer science, chemistry, and more. This module serves as a prerequisite to the others - it introduces the fundamentals of quantum computing and how to use Qiskit to run quantum circuits.\n"
msgstr "Qiskit in the Classroomモジュールでは、量子コンピューターを使って、量子力学・コンピューターサイエンス・化学などの量子コンピューティングに関連する様々な分野の概念を探求する機会があります。このモジュールは他のモジュールの前提条件として機能しており、量子コンピューティングの基礎と、Qiskitを使って量子Circuit（回路）を実行する方法を紹介します。\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "First we'll give you a brief overview of how a classical computer works, then we'll show you how these concepts are adapted to fit the quantum computing paradigm. Finally, we'll show you how to put these concepts together to build and execute your first quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr "まず古典的なコンピューターの仕組みについて簡単に説明し、次にこれらの概念が量子コンピューティングのパラダイムにどのように適用されるかを示します。最後に、これらの概念をまとめて最初の量子Circuit（回路）を構築・実行する方法をお見せします。\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Classical computers {#classical-computers}"
msgstr "古典的なコンピューター {#classical-computers}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "You're likely familiar with the basics of how classical computers work, but here we'll highlight a few of the key features so that we can then draw a comparison to quantum computers.\n"
msgstr "古典的なコンピューターの仕組みの基礎はすでにご存知かもしれませんが、ここでは量子コンピューターとの比較ができるよう、主要な特徴をいくつか取り上げます。\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The basic units of information: bits {#the-basic-units-of-information-bits}"
msgstr "情報の基本単位：ビット {#the-basic-units-of-information-bits}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Classical computers process classical information, and the fundamental unit of classical information is the *bit*. A single bit can store the answer to one \"yes/no\" question. We usually represent the two binary states of a bit as \"0\" and \"1\".\n"
msgstr "古典的なコンピューターは古典的な情報を処理しており、古典的な情報の基本単位は*ビット*です。1つのビットは「はい/いいえ」という1つの問いへの答えを格納できます。ビットの2つの二進状態は通常「0」と「1」で表されます。\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ####
#, no-wrap
msgid "Review of binary numbers {#review-of-binary-numbers}"
msgstr "二進数の復習 {#review-of-binary-numbers}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combining bits enables you to store more information. For example, if you want to store a number from 0 to 15, you could do so with four bits in the following way:\n"
msgstr "ビットを組み合わせることで、より多くの情報を格納できます。例えば、0から15の数値を格納したい場合、次のように4ビットで表現できます：\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|  | |  | |\n"
"| :------- | :------: | :-------: | -------: |\n"
"| 0 = 0000     |4 = 0100  | 8 = 1000    | 12 = 1100 |\n"
"| 1 = 0001  | 5 = 0101  | 9 = 1001  | 13 = 1101 |\n"
"| 2 = 0010  | 6 = 0110  | 10 = 1010   | 14 = 1110 |\n"
"| 3 = 0011  | 7 = 0111   | 11 = 1011  | 15 = 1111|\n"
msgstr ""
"|  | |  | |\n"
"| :------- | :------: | :-------: | -------: |\n"
"| 0 = 0000     |4 = 0100  | 8 = 1000    | 12 = 1100 |\n"
"| 1 = 0001  | 5 = 0101  | 9 = 1001  | 13 = 1101 |\n"
"| 2 = 0010  | 6 = 0110  | 10 = 1010   | 14 = 1110 |\n"
"| 3 = 0011  | 7 = 0111   | 11 = 1011  | 15 = 1111|\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In general, to convert from a binary number of $N$ bits to a familiar, base-10 number, you multiply the least-significant (rightmost) bit by $2^0 = 1$, the next bit to the left by $2^1 = 2$, then the next by $2^2 = 4$, and so on, until you reach the most significant (leftmost bit), which you multiply $2^{N-1}$.\n"
msgstr "一般に、$N$ビットの二進数を馴染みのある10進数に変換するには、最下位（最右端）のビットを$2^0 = 1$で乗算し、その左のビットを$2^1 = 2$で、次のビットを$2^2 = 4$で乗算し、最上位（最左端）のビットに達するまで$2^{N-1}$で乗算するまで続けます。\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "So, that means that $N$ bits can be in one of $2^N$ different possible states.\n"
msgstr "つまり、$N$ビットは$2^N$通りの異なる状態のうちの1つになり得ます。\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Check your understanding {#check-your-understanding}"
msgstr "理解度チェック {#check-your-understanding}"

# doq-bootstrap: dropped, JSX/HTML tags differ from English: <Accordion>
# <AccordionItem title="答え">
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "How many bits would you need to represent the number 86? Write out the bitstring that encodes this number in binary.\n"
msgstr ""

# doq-bootstrap: dropped, JSX/HTML tags differ from English: $N$ビットでは$0$から$2^N
# - 1$までの数を表せることを思い出してください。6ビットでは最大$2^6 - 1
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Accordion>\n"
"<AccordionItem title=\"Answer\">\n"
msgstr ""

# doq: emptied 2026-09-05, previous translation failed check.py
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Remember, $N$ bits allows you to represent the numbers $0$ through $2^N - 1$, so using six bits would get us up to $2^6 - 1 = 63$. That's not quite enough. We add one more bit to get up to $2^7 - 1 = 127$. Now let's break 86 down into powers of 2:\n"
msgstr ""

